Cyclomatic complexity
The author of Cyclomatic Complexity Metric is Thomas J. McCabe. He defines the complexity
of a software function according to the number of code paths available (graph nodes). The
more paths you have, the more complex your function is. McCabe considers that each
function must have a complexity score of less than 10. That means that, if the code has more
complex methods, you have to refactor it, transforming parts of these codes into separate
methods. There are some real scenarios where this behavior is easily detected:
